ccepted as a dataset-specific association. This is the strongest new stratum result, but the exposed cell is only just above the minimum-count requirement and the interval is wide. From a critical-reviewer perspective, it should not be interpreted as proof of uniform transportability or as independent confirmation; the pattern is compatible with a feature_050 modifier but could also reflect adaptive subgroup selection or unmeasured structure. H254/R249 in the feature_050=0 complement gives signed estimate +0.0545 with bounds -0.0134 to +0.1225 and cell counts 143/643. The direction is favorable but imprecise, leaving it unresolved. H253 and H254 motivate a direct feature_050 interaction test, which is the most informative follow-up because the observed stratum difference is large relative to the aggregate effect.\n\nH255/R250 tested feature_020 against outcome_006 within the more robust feature_002=1, feature_006=0, feature_011=0 population and age feature_061>=54. The signed estimate is +0.1324 with bounds +0.0225 to +0.2422 and cell counts 62/241. It clears the practical threshold, so H255 is accepted as a dataset-specific older-age stratum result. H256/R251 for age feature_061<=53 gives signed estimate +0.0609 with bounds -0.0525 to +0.1743 and cell counts 59/243. The lower bound fails, so H256 is unresolved. These results do not establish age modification. They are better read as an older-age localized confirmation of the robust feature_020/outcome_006 signal, while the younger-age stratum remains inconclusive because of precision and selection risk.\n\nH257/R252 tested feature_020 against outcome_006 within the same robust population but restricted to feature_060=1. The signed estimate is +0.1197 with bounds +0.0196 to +0.2199 and cell counts 76/316. This clears the practical threshold and supports the feature_006=1 localization within the robust population. H258/R253 for feature_060=0 gives signed estimate +0.0998 with bounds -0.0052 to +0.2047 and cell counts